arXiv:2509.08151v1 [cs.AI] 09 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Trust Semantics Distillation for Collaborator Selection via Memory-Augmented Agentic AI</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Botao Zhu
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">   </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jeslyn Wang
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">   </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Dusit Niyato
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">   </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xianbin Wang
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text">
B. Zhu and X. Wang (Corresponding author) are with Western University, Canada. J. Wang is with the University of Toronto, Canada. D. Niyato is with Nanyang Technological University, Singapore.
</span></span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Accurate trustworthiness evaluation of potential collaborating devices is essential for the effective execution of complex computing tasks. This evaluation process involves collecting diverse trust-related data from potential collaborators, including historical performance and available resources, for collaborator selection. However, when each task owner independently assesses all collaborators’ trustworthiness, frequent data exchange, complex reasoning, and dynamic situation changes can result in significant overhead and deteriorated trust evaluation. To overcome these challenges, we propose a task-specific trust semantics distillation (2TSD) model based on a large AI model (LAM)-driven teacher-student agent architecture. The teacher agent is deployed on a server with powerful computational capabilities and an augmented memory module dedicated to multidimensional trust-related data collection, task-specific trust semantics extraction, and task-collaborator matching analysis. Upon receiving task-specific requests from device-side student agents, the teacher agent transfers the trust semantics of potential collaborators to the student agents, enabling rapid and accurate collaborator selection. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed 2TSD model can reduce collaborator evaluation time, decrease device resource consumption, and improve the accuracy of collaborator selection.</span></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the growing complexity of computational applications over connected systems, individual resource-constrained devices become increasingly incompetent to execute complex computing tasks independently due to limited computational resources and capabilities. To address these limitations, a new collaborative computing paradigm, in which devices coordinate and share resources for collaborative task completion, has become a viable direction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. By leveraging distributed resources, this mechanism enhances the system’s task completion capabilities and operational efficiency. Due to its advantages, distributed collaborative computing has been widely adopted across domains such as smart manufacturing, smart cities, and e-health, providing significant benefits including reduced latency and improved resource utilization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To achieve effective task completion, the selection of reliable collaborators is a critical prerequisite. Previous studies have developed different approaches to assess a collaborator’s reliability by analyzing its social attributes, historical behaviors, and reputation metrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
Although these approaches offer partial insights, they often fall short of providing a situational-dependent assessment of collaborator trustworthiness, particularly with respect to task-specific considerations. Recently, trust has been adopted as an effective means for collaborator evaluation and selection. In this context, trust is defined as the expectation of a resource-constrained task owner on a potential collaborator’s capabilities, resources, and willingness for collaborative task completion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Trust evaluation of potential collaborators, which involves trust-related data collection and analysis, thus becomes an essential step of collaborator selection and task completion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, trust consists of inherently complex indicators that fluctuates with time and collaboration context. Existing trust evaluation and device labeling methods fail to capture the temporal dynamics and multi-dimensional characteristics of trust. Specifically, a device may exhibit completely distinct trust under changed collaboration tasks, within different time windows, or across various performance metrics. Consequently, a more comprehensive and dynamic trust evaluation mechanism is needed to characterize the evolving and task-specific device trust. In this study, we propose a new concept of trust semantics, which refers to collaborator evaluation across multiple dimensions—such as communication and computation domains—to capture their temporal evolution for an aggregated trust assessment. Compared with trustworthy or untrustworthy labels, our trust semantics can accurately capture the comprehensive trust and reflect the varying trustworthiness of devices in dynamic collaboration environments. However, accurately evaluating trust semantics through orchestrated data gathering and analysis under realistic conditions for collaborator selection faces several challenges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A primary challenge arises from the overhead of collecting diverse trust-related data from all potential collaborators. Frequent data exchange among these devices generates a massive volume of data, which incurs a significant burden and processing delay. Additionally, due to memory constraints, individual devices cannot store these data for all potential collaborators. To mitigate this challenge, a practical solution is to deploy a server within the collaborative system as the trust evaluation center, leveraging its ample computational and storage resources to collect and store trust-related data from all devices.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The second challenge lies in the difficulty of effectively assessing the trust semantics of collaborators due to the complexity of the collected trust-related data. These data are gathered from many devices, exhibiting multi-source and heterogeneous characteristics that require extremely complex processing, including consistency verification, feature alignment, and semantic fusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. In addition, computational resource-related trust information must be analyzed in a task-specific context to accurately reflect a device’s collaboration potential. Consequently, advanced trust modeling and reasoning tools are required to integrate multi-source heterogeneous data and interpret their trust semantic relationships, thus enabling effective extraction of device trust semantics. Large AI models (LAMs), with their powerful semantic understanding, multimodal data fusion, and reasoning capabilities, are well-suited for extracting critical insights from complex data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, making them promising tools for trust semantics extraction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The third challenge stems from the continuously evolving nature of both the collaborating system environment and task-specific requirements. Available resources on a device constantly fluctuate due to operational status and network conditions, which requires dynamic trust assessment mechanisms to capture these changes promptly for accurate evaluation results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, task requirements may shift due to different objectives, necessitating adaptive trust evaluation for task-specific assessments of collaborators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Leveraging their capabilities for continuous learning and autonomous adaptation, LAM-enabled agentic AI can perceive environmental changes, making them suitable for enabling dynamic trust evaluation in evolving collaborative systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To address the above challenges, this study proposes a novel task-specific trust semantics distillation (2TSD) model based on LAM-enabled agentic AI and a teacher-student agent architecture. The main contributions are summarized as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">• We propose a new concept of trust semantics to characterize the temporal evolution of devices across multiple dimensions, providing a more accurate and task-specific assessment of device trustworthiness. Additionally, we utilize LAM-enabled agentic AI for accurate extraction of trust semantics from complex trust-related data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">• We propose a teacher–student agent architecture for the first time for a server-based trust evaluation. The server-side teacher agent leverages comprehensive global data to extract task-specific trust semantics for devices and conduct task-collaborator matching analysis. The acquired knowledge is transferred to device-side student agents for lightweight collaborator selection. This architecture significantly reduces device resource consumption while enhancing the system’s adaptability in dynamic environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">• We design an augmented memory module for the teacher agent, enabling task-specific trust semantics to be effectively stored, updated, and retrieved, thereby supporting student agents in rapidly performing collaborator selection decisions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Agentic AI-Aided Teacher-Student Architecture for Trust Semantics Evaluation</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LAM-driven agentic AI, endowed with autonomous intelligence, advanced reasoning, and goal-oriented execution, can effectively perform diverse tasks in complex environments. Individual agents with independent decision-making capabilities can form adaptive multi-agent systems, enabling cooperative task execution through coordinated interactions with other agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Building on these strengths, we propose the state-of-the-art 2TSD model based on the teacher-student agent architecture, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ II Agentic AI-Aided Teacher-Student Architecture for Trust Semantics Evaluation ‣ Trust Semantics Distillation for Collaborator Selection via Memory-Augmented Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. This model employs LAM-enabled agentic AI to perform trust semantics evaluation and transfer between a trust server, acting as the teacher agent, and task owners, serving as student agents, for task-specific collaborator selection. The following analysis systematically examines the structure, advantages, and strategic value of the proposed framework.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08151v1/system.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:401/390;" width="401" height="390" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>A teacher-student architecture powered by LAM-enabled agentic AI for evaluating and transferring trust semantics between a trust server and task owners to enable task-specific collaborator selection.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LAM-Enabled Agentic AI for Trust Semantics Evaluation</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Benefiting from LAMs’ capabilities in multi-modal information integration, deep semantic representation, and complex reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, LAM-enabled agentic AI could provide several key advantages for trust semantics evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evolving Trust Evaluation via Continuous Interaction</span>.
Since device performance and task requirements often fluctuate, agentic AI can continuously interact with others to accumulate knowledge and learn from past collaborative experiences. This effectively enables adaptive trust assessments that track real-time variations in both devices and tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-Specific Trust Evaluation via Context-Aware Decision-Making</span>.
With strong context-awareness, agentic AI can autonomously process collected device performance data, handling both explicit information, such as device states, and implicit information, including collaborators’ behavioral patterns, to extract task-specific trust semantics. In addition, it can intelligently conduct task-collaborator matching analysis, enabling task-specific collaborator selection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accurate Trust Semantic Extraction</span>. Trust-related data are collected from diverse devices and span multiple heterogeneous dimensions, necessitating effective fusion.
Leveraging advanced cross-modal semantic understanding, agentic AI can integrate complex and heterogeneous data and extract precise trust semantics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Teacher-Student Agent Architecture</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Due to their limited computational resources, devices are not suitable for complex trust semantics extraction in dynamic environments. To address this, a server equipped with the LAM-enabled teacher agent is proposed to collect global trust-related data, extract trust semantics, and transfer the acquired knowledge to device-side student agents, enabling them to perform lightweight collaborator selection. This setup forms a teacher–student agent architecture between the server and the devices, whose specific advantages are outlined below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unified Trust Evaluation Standard</span>. By possessing global trust-related data, the server-side agent can evaluate all devices using a unified assessment standard, thereby eliminating inconsistencies that may arise from local observation differences or independent evaluation by student agents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reduced Communication and Computation Overhead</span>.
By reporting trust-related data solely to the server, devices avoid massive inter-device data exchange and the need for complex local trust evaluations, substantially reducing both communication overhead and computational load.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rapid Collaborator Selection</span>.
The teacher agent, leveraging its computational capabilities and the augmented memory module, can rapidly respond to devices’ task requests by providing precise task-specific trust semantics. The student agents then utilize this knowledge to perform lightweight local decisions, enabling fast collaborator selection.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08151v1/teacher.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:843/343;" width="843" height="343" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Workflow of the server-side memory-augmented teacher agent: resource information storage, historical record storage, and trust semantics extraction and storage.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Task-Specific Trust Semantics Distillation</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section details the implementation of the 2TSD model, including the system model, the extraction and storage of task-specific trust semantics, and their transfer to the task owner for collaborator selection.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">System Model</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a collaborative system consisting of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> devices <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{1},\dots,a_{I}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{1},\dots,a_{I}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and a server <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>. An LAM-enabled teacher agent is deployed on the server, while each device hosts an LAM-enabled student agent.
Every device can act as a task owner to generate a computational task, which is defined by its physical attributes. For example, device <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> generates a face recognition task consisting of a set of images, represented as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> = {“task type” : “face recognition”, “size” : “100 MB”, “processing density” : “2,339 cycles/bit”, “maximum task completion tolerance time” : “60 seconds”}. For elaboration purposes, we assume that the system includes three task types: facial recognition, video transcoding, and text word count <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. If device <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is selected by device <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to execute task <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the server <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> monitors their collaboration and records the relevant performance data of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> = {“task owner” : “<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>”, “collaborator” : “<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>”, “task type” : “face recognition”, “collaboration time” : “2025-08-18 14:00:00”, “throughput” : “100 Mbps”, “packet loss rate” : “1%”, “task processing speed” : “2 MB/second”, “accuracy” : “98%”, “comprehensive result” : “satisfied”}.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Task-Specific Trust Semantics Extraction and Storage</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The server-side teacher agent, acting as the central authority for trust decisions, collects and stores trust-related data, including historical performance records and resource information. It then extracts, updates, and maintains task-specific trust semantics for devices, which are subsequently provided to student agents.
Due to LAM’s limited internal memory, an external memory module is required to support storage and efficient retrieval. Therefore, we design the memory module with three distinct components: <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i) Resource information storage component</span> stores the physical resource information of all devices; <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ii) Historical record storage component</span> maintains historical performance data of all devices. <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">iii) Trust semantics storage component</span> stores task-specific trust semantics of devices derived from historical performance data. Next, we present a detailed description of the teacher agent’s workflow, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-B Teacher-Student Agent Architecture ‣ II Agentic AI-Aided Teacher-Student Architecture for Trust Semantics Evaluation ‣ Trust Semantics Distillation for Collaborator Selection via Memory-Augmented Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, highlighting how it uses the memory module.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Resource Information Storage</span>. Each device reports its resources under the idle condition, including CPU, storage, bandwidth, and so on, to the server. To maintain up-to-date information, the device promptly notifies the server whenever its idle-state resources change. Note that resource changes occurring during task execution do not need to be synchronized with the server. In the resource information storage component, the resource information of each device is stored in a key–value format, where the key corresponds to the device and the value represents its resources.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Historical Record Storage</span>. After a pair of devices completes a collaborative task, the server receives the collaborator’s performance data, such as <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and stores it in the historical record storage component of the memory module. This component is implemented through a relational database, such as MySQL, where each record is stored in a structured format that includes task owner, collaborator, task type, collaboration timestamp, and associated communication and computation performance metrics.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08151v1/teacher_student.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:420/544;" width="420" height="544" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Task-specific trust semantics transfer from the teacher agent to the student agent for collaborator selection.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-Specific Trust Semantics Extraction</span>.
After storing a device’s historical performance data, the teacher agent promptly extracts and stores its task-specific trust semantics. As shown in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II-B Teacher-Student Agent Architecture ‣ II Agentic AI-Aided Teacher-Student Architecture for Trust Semantics Evaluation ‣ Trust Semantics Distillation for Collaborator Selection via Memory-Augmented Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, based on <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{a_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{a_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the teacher agent retrieves device <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>’s historical records for face recognition task type within a specified time period from the historical record component, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{a_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{a_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and feeds them into the LAM to extract the task-specific trust semantics for device <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The trust semantics comprises three parts: the device’s overall trust status, the temporal variations of communication-related trust data, and the temporal variations of computation-related trust data. The extracted trust semantics is represented as “{“device” : “<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>”, “task_type” : “face recognition”, “trust_semantics (<math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\text{fr}}_{a_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext>fr</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\text{fr}}_{a_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math>)” : {“state” : “trusted”, “communication-related” : “packet loss rate shows a normal trend, throughput shows a decreasing trend”, “computation-related” : “accuracy shows a normal trend, task processing speed shows a normal trend”}, …}”.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-Specific Trust Semantics Storage</span>. To enable flexible management of trust semantics, we design a tree-structured storage module with three hierarchical layers: task type, device, and trust semantics. In this structure, all task types, devices, and task-specific trust semantics are represented as nodes, with the root node <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> serving as the starting point without semantic meaning. Each node is expressed as a triplet <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>self, parent, child<math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>, supporting structured storage and traversal operations.
For instance, given the trust semantics extracted previously, the teacher agent first checks whether the face recognition node already exists in the tree. If absent, a new face recognition node is created. If present, the teacher agent then checks whether the device node <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is present under this task type. If absent, a new <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> node is inserted along with its trust semantics as a child node. If node <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> already exists, its trust semantics child node will be updated. Each device currently maintains a single trust semantics entry per task type, which can be extended to support multiple temporal entries for the same task type.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It should be noted that the teacher agent repeatedly performs the above steps to maintain up-to-date task-specific trust semantics for each device.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Task-Specific Trust Semantics Transfer from Teacher to Student</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When a device initiates a new task, the teacher agent transmits the stored task-specific trust semantics of potential collaborators, along with the corresponding task-collaborator matching analysis, to the student agent on that device, as shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ III-B Task-Specific Trust Semantics Extraction and Storage ‣ III Task-Specific Trust Semantics Distillation ‣ Trust Semantics Distillation for Collaborator Selection via Memory-Augmented Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The student agent then utilizes this information to rapidly select an appropriate collaborator. For example, device <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> generates a new task <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> = {“task type” : “video transcoding”, “size” : “50 MB”, “processing density” : “1,000 cycles/bit”, “maximum task completion tolerance time” : “50 seconds”} and sends the request, “I have a task <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> = {…}, could you help me identify some trusted collaborators?”, to the server.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task-Specific Trust Semantics Retrieval</span>. Upon receiving a task request, the teacher agent quickly locates the corresponding task type node within its tree-structured trust semantics storage component. It then retrieves all child and grandchild nodes of that task type node to obtain the potential collaborators and their associated trust semantics. For task <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the retrieved relevant information is represented as <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{c_{2}}^{\text{ts}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mtext>ts</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{c_{2}}^{\text{ts}}</annotation></semantics></math> = {{“device” : “<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>”, “task_type” : “video transcoding”, “trust semantics (<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{a_{k}}^{\text{vt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mtext>vt</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{a_{k}}^{\text{vt}}</annotation></semantics></math>)” : …}, {“device” : “<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>”,…}, {“device” : “<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>”,…}…}.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Resource Information Retrieval</span>. For the potential collaborators obtained in the previous step, the teacher agent queries their resources from the resource information storage component. Based on <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{c_{2}}^{\text{ts}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mtext>ts</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{c_{2}}^{\text{ts}}</annotation></semantics></math>, all retrieved devices and their resource information are represented as <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\text{re}}_{c_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mtext>re</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\text{re}}_{c_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> = {{“device” : “<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>”, “CPU” : “2.91 GHz”…}, {“device” : “<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>”,…}, {“device” : “<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>”,…}…}.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation of Task-Collaborator Matching via Chain-of-Trust</span>. Evaluating whether potential collaborators possess sufficient resources to complete a task effectively is a key aspect of trust assessment. Given the complexity of this evaluation, it is carried out by the teacher agent to guarantee accuracy. The teacher agent inputs <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M^{\text{re}}_{c_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mtext>re</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M^{\text{re}}_{c_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and task <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> into the LAM, which performs the task-collaborator matching analysis for each device. To reduce computational complexity and improve evaluation precision, a chain-of-trust mechanism is employed.
This mechanism decomposes the evaluation into multiple chained stages aligned with task requirements, with the output of each stage serving as the input for the next. The final stage produces the aggregated evaluation result. Detailed implementation of the chain-of-trust mechanism is provided in our prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. For task <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, devices <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are identified as meeting the task requirements, denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{c_{2}}^{\text{mt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mtext>mt</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{c_{2}}^{\text{mt}}</annotation></semantics></math> = {{“device” : “<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>”, “task-collaborator matching” : “yes”}, {“device” : “<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>”, “task-collaborator matching” : “yes”}}.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Selection of Potential Collaborators</span>. Based on <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{c_{2}}^{\text{ts}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mtext>ts</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{c_{2}}^{\text{ts}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{c_{2}}^{\text{mt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mtext>mt</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{c_{2}}^{\text{mt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the teacher agent selects potential collaborators that are both trustworthy and meet the task requirements. The results in <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{c_{2}}^{\text{mt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mtext>mt</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{c_{2}}^{\text{mt}}</annotation></semantics></math> are then integrated into devices’ trust semantics, denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{c_{2}}^{\text{pc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mtext>pc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{c_{2}}^{\text{pc}}</annotation></semantics></math> = {{“device” : “<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>”, “task_type” : “video transcoding”, “trust semantics (<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{a_{k}}^{\text{vt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mtext>vt</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{a_{k}}^{\text{vt}}</annotation></semantics></math>)” : {“state” : “trusted”, “communication-related” : “packet loss rate shows a normal trend, throughput shows a normal trend”, “computation-related” : “accuracy shows a normal trend, task processing speed shows a normal trend”, “task-collaborator matching” : “yes”}}, {“device” : “<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>”, “task_type” : “video transcoding”, “trust semantics (<math id="S3.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{a_{j}}^{\text{vt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext>vt</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{a_{j}}^{\text{vt}}</annotation></semantics></math>)” : {“state” : “trusted”, “communication-related” : “packet loss rate shows a increasing trend, throughput shows a normal trend”, “computation-related” : “accuracy shows a normal trend, task processing speed shows a normal trend”, “task-collaborator matching” : “yes”}}}. Subsequently, the teacher agent sends <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{c_{2}}^{\text{pc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>M</mi><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mtext>pc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{c_{2}}^{\text{pc}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the student agent on device <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lightweight Decision-Making for Final Collaborator Selection</span>.
Upon receiving information from the teacher agent, the student agent selects the final collaborator through a lightweight decision-making process based on its own objectives, preferences, or other strategies. For example, device <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> ultimately selects device <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> as the collaborator for task <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Although both candidate devices <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are evaluated as trustworthy, device <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is excluded from the final selection due to an increasing packet loss rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed 2TSD model effectively reduces communication and computational overhead on devices while improving the accuracy, timeliness, and adaptability of task-specific trust evaluation, thereby providing a robust foundation for collaborator selection in dynamic environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experimental Analysis</span></h2>

<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08151v1/spend_time.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:408/242;" width="408" height="242" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>2TSD reduces collaborator evaluation time compared with baseline methods by leveraging trust semantics stored in the memory module of the teacher agent.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the proposed approach, we create a collaborative system comprising 9 Google Pixel 8 smartphones and a Dell 5200 server. We implement all agents in the system using GPT-4o-mini. Agents interact with GPT-4o-mini via API calls, while devices communicate over Wi-Fi.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV Experimental Analysis ‣ Trust Semantics Distillation for Collaborator Selection via Memory-Augmented Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents a comparison of collaborator evaluation times, defined as the interval between task generation by the task owner and the completion of collaborator selection.
As the number of devices increases, the collaborator evaluation times of the trust model with fitness-based clustering scheme (TMFSC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and the 2TSD without a memory module rise. This is because these methods require additional time for trust-related data collection and trust evaluation. The 2TSD without a memory module takes slightly more time than TMFSC due to the inference overhead of the LAM. In contrast, the collaborator evaluation time of the proposed 2TSD method remains almost constant, as it only needs to extract stored task-specific trust semantics from the memory module and perform the task-collaborator matching analysis, thus significantly accelerating collaborator selection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ IV Experimental Analysis ‣ Trust Semantics Distillation for Collaborator Selection via Memory-Augmented Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> compares the number of data collections, calculated as the historical performance data collection events that occur on devices. The 2TSD model requires fewer collections than TMFSC because its teacher agent uses the augmented memory module to store collaborators’ historical performance records. Consequently, during collaborator selection for each task, 2TSD avoids redundant data collection from potential collaborators, substantially reducing device-side communication and computational overhead. In contrast, TMFSC without a memory module must collect these data from potential collaborators for every task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F6" title="Fig. 6 ‣ IV Experimental Analysis ‣ Trust Semantics Distillation for Collaborator Selection via Memory-Augmented Agentic AI" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> compares collaborator selection accuracy. It can be observed that 2TSD significantly outperforms TMFSC, which can be attributed to two factors. First, the server in 2TSD maintains historical performance data for all devices, enabling the extraction of more accurate task-specific trust semantics. Second, leveraging LAM for task-collaborator matching analysis enhances matching precision, thereby improving the accuracy of collaborator selection.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08151v1/collection_count.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:408/242;" width="408" height="242" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 5: </span>2TSD achieves fewer data collections compared with TMFSC as its teacher agent uses the augmented memory module to store collaborators’ historical performance records.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08151v1/accuracy.png" id="S4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:408/238;" width="408" height="238" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 6: </span>2TSD improves the accuracy of collaborator selection compared with TMFSC due to precise trust semantics extraction and task-collaborator matching analysis.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Future Directions</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The concept of trust semantics presents many promising research directions, with numerous opportunities for further exploration alongside the 2TSD model, particularly regarding their applications in realistic environments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation and Mitigation of Environmental Changes on Dynamic Trust</span>. Future connected systems could face extremely dynamic operational environments. This is mainly due to dynamics in computing and communication resources as well as operational environments. By leveraging an LAM’s capabilities in environmental perception and semantic understanding, the influence of environmental factors on dynamic trust can be characterized more precisely.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Predictive Trust Distillation</span>. Given the complexity and inevitable processing delays, trust semantics evaluation and exchange in collaborative systems could take a significant amount of time–leading to reduced relevancy of trust evaluation due to evolving task requirements. To mitigate the undesired impact, predictive trust evaluation mechanisms could be incorporated into 2TSD for anticipated potential trust semantics, thereby enhancing the foresight and adaptability of decision-making on collaborator selection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">• <span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Trust Semantics Extraction Under Data-Missing Scenarios</span>. The current communication infrastructure experiences lots of difficulty in achieving deterministic and guaranteed data exchange. As a result, packet and data losses are unavoidable between the teacher and student agents in the proposed trust semantics distillation process. Given that trust-related data may be incomplete or missing in practice, accurately extracting trust semantics under realistic conditions of data sparsity or missingness constitutes an important research direction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we have proposed an innovative agentic-AI based teacher-student architecture for trust semantics distillation, aimed at achieving resource-efficient, rapid, and accurate collaborator selection in dynamic systems. The teacher agent deployed at the server side extracts and maintains task-specific trust semantics while conducting task-collaborator matching analysis. Through knowledge transfer from the teacher agent, device-side student agents are enabled to execute lightweight collaborator selection decisions locally. The proposed model significantly reduces computational and communication overhead on devices, facilitates rapid decision-making, and enhances the accuracy of collaborator selection by leveraging task-specific trust semantics. Furthermore, its scalable and robust architecture ensures reliability and adaptability in complex dynamic environments, providing a solid technical foundation for intelligent collaborative decision-making.</p>
</div>
</section>
